language ESSENCE' 1.0
$ csplib_044_steiner: an Essence' model from DCP Rosetta.
$
$ The ternary Steiner problem of order n is to find a set of n * (n-1) / 6 triples of distinct elements from 1 to n such that any two triples have at most one common element. It is a hypergraph problem from combinatorial mathematics, where n modulo 6 must be equal to 1 or 3.
$
$ Save this file as model.eprime. Download `csplib_044_steiner.json` and `dcp_convert_essence.py` from the
$ problem's page:
$   https://dcp-bench.github.io/DCP-Rosetta/problems/csplib_044_steiner.html
$ Convert the instances once, then run the first one with Savile Row 1.11 (it needs Java); the
$ solution is written to `csplib_044_steiner_1.param.solution`:
$   python dcp_convert_essence.py csplib_044_steiner.json
$   savilerow model.eprime csplib_044_steiner_1.param -run-solver

$ n: the order of the Steiner system, so the elements are 1 to n
given n : int(1..)
$ the number of triples, n * (n-1) / 6
letting triples = n * (n - 1) / 6

$ sets[r,j]: element j is part of triple r (rows and columns 1-based)
find sets : matrix indexed by [int(1..triples), int(1..n)] of bool

branching on [sets]
such that
$ each triple consists of exactly three distinct elements
forAll r : int(1..triples) . ((sum j : int(1..n) . toInt(sets[r,j])) = 3),
$ any two distinct triples have at most one common element
forAll r : int(1..triples) . forAll s : int(r+1..triples) . ((sum j : int(1..n) . toInt(sets[r,j] /\ sets[s,j])) <= 1)
